<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The parties married in April 2014. The wife alleged dowry demands, harassment and ouster from the matrimonial home, and filed a DV petition in 2018 alongside a police complaint. In the pending DV case, she applied under Section 20 for the child&rsquo;s educational expenses for the academic year 2025-26 — roughly Rs 97,500 towards fees, books and transport.</p>
<p>The Magistrate rejected the application in September 2025, principally because interim maintenance was already being paid. The mother appealed under Section 29. She also alleged that the father had remarried and had substantial income; the appellate court left those contested questions to the trial court.</p>

<h2>Interim maintenance is not a complete answer</h2>
<p>The appellate court identified the flaw in the Magistrate&rsquo;s order precisely: it treated the existence of interim maintenance as a complete answer, without ever examining the nature and quantum of the school demand. Section 20(1)(d) expressly contemplates monetary relief for the maintenance of the aggrieved person <em>and her children</em>, and Section 20(2) requires the relief to be adequate, fair and reasonable. A specific, documented educational expense is a distinct head; what the court must prevent is double recovery, not the claim itself.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;Education is not a luxury or an optional expenditure&rdquo; &mdash; and &ldquo;The child is not responsible for the matrimonial differences between his parents.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<h2>How the share was fixed</h2>
<p>Two strands of Supreme Court authority did the work. From <em>Gaurav Nagpal v. Sumedha Nagpal</em>, the welfare of the child as the governing consideration; from <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>, the income-based, affidavit-driven approach to quantifying support. Both parents are responsible for the child, the court held, but contributions need not be equal — capacity matters. On the material available, it fixed the father&rsquo;s share at fifty per cent of the actual, established expenses, payable for 2025-26 within thirty days of the school demand being produced before the Magistrate, with credit for amounts already paid. The direction operates until the main case is decided and is modifiable; the mother&rsquo;s prayer that the father bear the whole amount was rejected.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For claimant mothers.</strong> Put the school demand itself on record — fee circulars, book lists, transport charges. A Section 20 claim for education succeeds on documents, and fails on generalities.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For respondent fathers.</strong> The answer to an inflated claim is proof of capacity and proof of payments already made, not a bare objection that maintenance is being paid. Credit for existing payments was expressly preserved here.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For Delhi practice.</strong> The same architecture — Section 20(1)(d) read with Rajnesh affidavits — governs applications before Delhi&rsquo;s Mahila Courts. Specific heads such as education, medical expenses and school transport can be claimed as such, alongside or after interim maintenance.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Monetary relief under the DV Act is often litigated as a single monthly figure, which obscures the statute&rsquo;s design: Section 20 authorises relief under distinct heads, including loss of earnings, medical expenses and maintenance of children. Courts across the country, applying <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>, have moved towards a documented, affidavit-based assessment of both sides&rsquo; means. This judgment adds a clean appellate statement that a running maintenance order does not foreclose a specific, proved educational expense — a point of immediate, everyday use to litigants on both sides of a DV case.</p>
